My current finding is that the limit of the ratio \(R_{2n}= \dfrac{CT\cdot NB}{BT\cdot NE}\) is **not** \(\dfrac{7}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt{13}}{2}\) (≈5.3028) as claimed in Finding 20.  Using high‑precision (150‑digit) mpmath for even indices up to \(n=40\) gives  

\[
R_{2}=2.469104212945\,,\;
R_{4}=2.626262834371\,,\;
R_{6}=2.650551047079\,,\;
R_{8}=2.654122608959\,,\;
R_{10}=2.654733408183\,,\;
R_{12}=2.654733414075\,,\;
R_{14}=2.654733414075\;,
\]

and the values stabilize at  

\[
\alpha=\lim_{n\to\infty}R_{2n}=2.654733414075\ldots .
\]

Thus \(\alpha\) is about \(2.654733414\), far from \(5.3027756\).  Consequently the rational‑plus‑square‑root representation must be different; the proposed \(p=\tfrac72,\;q=\tfrac{13}{4}\) is incorrect.  Any final answer based on that representation will be wrong.
